  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nMoisture associated with the remnants of an atmospheric river \nwill continue to bring widespread cloudiness, along with rain that\nwill change over to snow at all elevations overnight tonight. As \nthis precipitation changes over, it will likely do so around the \ntime a band of heavier precipitation pushes through, which makes \nthe forecast very sensitive to meso- and microscale phenomena that\nwill be described below in the Confidence and Scenarios section. \nOtherwise, as the rain changes over to snow overnight, warm \ndaytime temperatures and overnight temperatures just below \nfreezing will limit accumulations to elevated and grassy surfaces,\nwhich may make bridges and overpasses more hazardous as they will\nice up first. \n\nAs snow winds down on Wednesday, winds will pick up, which will \nresult in a brief window where blowing snow may become a concern. \nWith that said, the wet nature of the snow, along with \ntemperatures rising above freezing, should be just enough to keep \nthe blowing snow from becoming a widespread concern. \n\nThe series of weather systems moving into the Western US will \ncontinue through the remainder of the week, keeping us cool and \nunsettled. For the remainder of this week, expect mostly cloudy \nskies with a few light snow showers around as temperatures \ngenerally hover in the low to mid 30s for highs in the lower \nelevations, but as we head into next week precipitation may become\nmore widespread as more moisture rich systems approach from the \nPacific.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nSnowfall tonight: As mentioned above, the largest factor in how\nexactly the weather unfolds tonight will be determined by when\nexactly the precipitation changes over to snowfall, and models are\nstill showing a bit of a spread on this transition. Models that\nhave been trending on the cooler side, such as the HRRR, show this\ntransition occurring as or just after the heaviest precipitation\npushes through the area, which fits the mental model of dynamic\ncooling one would expect during these events. However, the RRFS\ntends to keep temperatures just warm enough for precipitation to\nfall as rain until behind this heavier band of precipitation, when\nsome cold air advection kicks in. Given that temperatures are\ngoing to be straddling the freezing mark with this event, both\nscenarios remain entirely plausible. Below is a more detailed\nbreakdown based on location. Ludwig\n\nSome areas to watch for moderate winter weather impacts \ntoday/tonight include:\n\nGlacier county...Where snow accumulation will come in two rounds \n(this morning and again later this afternoon/evening) and the \nprobability for total accumulation of 6 inches or more is as high\nas 60-70% at the Alberta border and 30-40% for areas along US-2 \nincluding Cut Bank, Browning and East Glacier. Coldest ground and \nair temperatures exist here with potential for road surface icing\nand some localized blowing/drifting snow tonight with the \ndevelopment of northwest winds gusting 20-30 mph. \n\nContinental Divide Passes from Rogers Pass south to Homestake...Where \nperiods of moderate to heavy wet snow are likely between 4pm-8pm. \nEnhanced snowfall rates of one half to one inch per hour may occur \nduring this time, leading to significant visibility reduction and \nquick snow accumulation on road surfaces.  \n\nThe I-15 corridor north of Helena through Great Falls and \nShelby...Where a N-S band of moderate to embedded heavier precipitation\nrates is likely to develop after 4pm and may persist for areas \nfrom Great Falls north to the border through much of the evening. \nThe occurrence of the transition to snow and onset of heavier \nsnowfall rates along the I-15 corridor may coincide with the \nevening commute and occur just after sunset with lower visibility \nand slushy road accumulations affecting travel, especially north \nof Great Falls. \n\nGallatin County from 7pm to around midnight...Where there is \nstill considerable uncertainly in the timing of the transition to \nsnow in the Gallatin valley, but it may occur with heavier \nprecipitation rates late this evening. A period of more intense \nsnowfall and snow/slush accumulation is likely over Bozeman Pass\nduring this period. Hoenisch",
